<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A trader mixes grade AB coffee at Ksh <m>{1250}</m> a kilogram with grade AA coffee at Ksh <m>{1800}</m> a kilogram so that the mixture costs Ksh <m>{1580}</m> a kilogram. Find the ratio of the masses, AB : AA, in its simplest form.</p><p><fillin characters="4" name="ans1"/> : <fillin characters="4" name="ans2"/>  </p><p/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Balance the distances from the target price. Each kilogram of AB is <m>{1580} - {1250} = {330}</m> shillings below the target; each kilogram of AA is <m>{1800} - {1580} = {220}</m> shillings above it. Cross the distances over:</p><p><me>\text{cheap} : \text{dear} = {220} : {330} = {2} : {3}</me></p><p>Check: <m>({2} \times {1250} + {3} \times {1800}) \div {5} = {1580}</m>. The target is nearer the dearer grade, so more of that grade is used.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>